<section>
  <h2>What happens to a photo after you clean it</h2>
  <p>The pixels stay identical. The surrounding data does not.</p>
  <p>
    Cleaning cuts whole metadata segments and copies everything else byte for byte, so the image looks
    and prints exactly the same. What changes is the file's declaration: device, time, place, software
    and any stored text are gone. The before-and-after table in your result lists each segment with its
    fate, and the report re-reads the output to confirm it. You can then
    <a href={LINKS.pricing.href}>see the free and paid plans</a> if you also need video or PDF later.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a photo shows no fields at all, treat that as absence of evidence, not as a verdict. Nothing
    found does not mean the file is not AI. Midjourney, for example, does not write C2PA or IPTC
    metadata as of early 2026, so its pictures usually have nothing to find; that is its writing
    behavior, not a detection failure, and removing metadata cannot change what pixels tell a detector.
  </p>
</section>
